   <text:p text:style-name="P91"><text:span text:style-name="T68">T</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T69">he hierarchical structure of nylon fibres. Figure reprinted from “</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T30">Use of scattering methods in chemical </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P92"><text:span text:style-name="T70">i</text:span>ndustry - SAXS and SANS from fibers and films”. Chapter 21, <text:span text:style-name="T71">in “</text:span>Neutrons, X-rays and Light: </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P92">Scattering Methods Applied to Soft Condensed Matter”. Lindner <text:span text:style-name="T70">&amp; </text:span>Zemb (editors), North-Holland. <text:span text:style-name="T71">2002.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P88"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P93">This periodicity in the nanostructure manifests itself as a peak in the scattering data.</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P93"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P94">At the bottom of the Data Explorer panel, click the <text:span text:style-name="T72">drop-down that currently says ‘Fitting’ and select </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T73">Cor</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T74">func</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T72">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T75">The Fit P</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T76">anel</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T75"> transforms into a C</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T77">orfunc Perspective</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T75"> page.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P94"/>
