   <text:p text:style-name="P125">At this point, in SasView 4.x/5.x, you would now perform the low- and high-Q extrapolations, before transforming the extrapolated data. And then, should you wish, get SasView to derive information from the <text:span text:style-name="T134">computed</text:span> correlation function. But t<text:span text:style-name="T134">hose</text:span> operations are <text:span text:style-name="T134">completed as</text:span> three separate steps. <text:span text:style-name="T59">In SasView 6.x the three </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T135">operations </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T136">(extrapolation &gt; transformation &gt; extraction)</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T59"> have been combined into one step!</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P126"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P127"><text:span text:style-name="T134">Just </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T137">like </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">in</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T134"> </text:span>SasView <text:span text:style-name="T137">4</text:span>.x/<text:span text:style-name="T137">5.x, SasView 6.x</text:span> <text:span text:style-name="T11">still </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T137">allows you to choose whether to have it automatically determine the background level and fit parameters for the Guinier and Porod extrapolations. Automatic determinations are selected by default.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P128"/>
