   <text:p text:style-name="P164"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P163"/>
   <table:table table:name="Table11" table:style-name="Table11">
    <table:table-column table:style-name="Table11.A"/>
    <table:table-row>
     <table:table-cell table:style-name="Table11.A1" office:value-type="string">
      <text:p text:style-name="P165"><text:span text:style-name="T161">Both are damped oscillatory functions with the maxima denoting each new lamellar repeat. But n</text:span>otice that less structure is evident in <text:span text:style-name="T201">Γ</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T202">3</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T203">(</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T204">z</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T203">) </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T147">because of the orientational averaging.</text:span></text:p>
      <text:p text:style-name="P165"/>
      <text:p text:style-name="P166">The reader may be familiar with pair distribution functions (usually abbreviated to PDF’s) from Total Scattering measurements. In the same way PDF’s describe the probability of finding two atoms separated by a given distance in a material, these correlation functions reflect the separation of similar SLD (and by inference, similar density).</text:p>
     </table:table-cell>
    </table:table-row>
   </table:table>
   <text:p text:style-name="P167"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P168"><text:span text:style-name="T205"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P169"><text:span text:style-name="T205"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P169"><text:span text:style-name="T205"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P169"><text:span text:style-name="T205"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P169"><text:span text:style-name="T205"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P169"><text:span text:style-name="T205"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P169"><text:span text:style-name="T205"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P169"><text:span text:style-name="T205"/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P170"><text:soft-page-break/><text:span text:style-name="T206">Now c</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T188">lick on the plot tab labelled ‘</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T207">IDF</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T190">’ </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T208">to see the Interface Distribution Function, </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T209">g</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T210">1</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T209">(z)</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T193">.</text:span></text:p>
